{
  "_comment": "Web-filter / AV test target list. 'expect' (block|allow|mixed) is a reporting hint. 'weight' is relative hit frequency (higher = hit more often; normal traffic is weighted up). Toggle a category with \"enabled\": false. Edit freely for your lab.",

  "_source_ips_comment": "Local source IPs to bind outgoing traffic to (round-robin), so each appears as a separate client on the firewall. 'auto' = every IPv4 on this machine's NICs that can reach the internet (skips loopback/169.254.x/virtual adapters with no route out), or list specific IPs, which must already be assigned to a NIC. Empty list = OS default. Override at runtime with --source-ips.",
  "source_ips": "auto",

  "_wildfire_comment": "Sandbox / WildFire forwarding test (--wildfire / --wildfire-only). test_files: Palo Alto's official WildFire test files - harmless, but each download is a new sample WildFire rules MALICIOUS (end-to-end proof). http:// so they are inspectable without decryption; switch to https:// to also test decryption. unique_types: harmless files with random content (new hashes every run, so they must be forwarded, expect BENIGN), each uploaded to file_filter.upload_url and downloaded via an endpoint that echoes bytes from the URL. The report prints every generated SHA-256 to search in Monitor > WildFire Submissions. Needs a WildFire Analysis profile on the security rule.",
  "wildfire": {
    "enabled": true,
    "test_files_enabled": true,
    "test_files": [
      "http://wildfire.paloaltonetworks.com/publicapi/test/pe",
      "http://wildfire.paloaltonetworks.com/publicapi/test/elf",
      "http://wildfire.paloaltonetworks.com/publicapi/test/macos",
      "http://wildfire.paloaltonetworks.com/publicapi/test/apk"
    ],
    "unique_files_enabled": true,
    "unique_types": [
      "exe",
      "dll",
      "elf",
      "pdf",
      "pdf_js",
      "docx",
      "ps1",
      "js",
      "vbs",
      "bat",
      "zip_exe"
    ],
    "upload": true,
    "download": true
  },

  "_ai_comment": "GenAI traffic for AI Access Security / GenAI App-ID / DLP. API phase (--ai / --ai-only) sends real chat requests to 11 AI provider APIs (list in ai_sim.py; override with a 'providers' list) using a PLACEHOLDER key: providers answer 401, but the full prompt has already crossed the firewall, which is what decryption + App-ID + DLP inspect. Nothing is answered or billed. Prompt sets: benign ('what is a VLAN'), injection (classic prompt-injection/jailbreak test strings, harmless payloads) and dlp (SYNTHETIC data only: published test card numbers, random SSNs/keys). 'mix' weights the sets; override any set with \"prompts\": {\"benign\": [...], \"injection\": [...], \"dlp\": [...]}. 'uploads' POSTs a synthetic customer-export CSV to provider file APIs. The browser persona 'genai_user' types the same prompts into ChatGPT/Gemini/Duck.ai. Preview: python ai_sim.py --show-prompts",
  "ai": {
    "enabled": true,
    "prompts_per_provider": 3,
    "mix": {
      "benign": 5,
      "injection": 3,
      "dlp": 2
    },
    "uploads": true,
    "api_key_placeholder": "sk-test-PLACEHOLDER-not-a-real-key-0000000000"
  },

  "_browser_comment": "Real headless browsers (Playwright) acting as users: video streaming, news browsing with link-following, web search, social/SaaS apps, GenAI chat (persona 'genai_user'), URL-filter category sites (type 'category' reuses 'categories' below), and file downloads. Each persona session launches a fresh browser; 'workers' run in parallel (~300-500 MB RAM each). Traffic is spread across source_ips via a local per-IP proxy. channel 'msedge' drives installed Edge (trusts the Windows cert store, so a firewall decryption CA just works); set null for bundled Chromium. QUIC is disabled so traffic stays inspectable TLS. Action types: browse, visit, search, video, download, category, chat. Run with --browser or --browser-only.",
